In the framework of constructive (Euclidean) quantum field theory, many models of interest possess important symmetries — for instance, local gauge symmetry (invariance under a compact or non-compact Lie group acting fiberwise on the fields), supersymmetry (a graded symmetry mixing bosonic and fermionic field components, often used to represent determinants or to implement disorder averaging), and replica symmetry (the permutation symmetry S_n acting on n identical copies of a system, used in the replica method for quenched disorder and typically requiring an n → 0 limit). Constructive analysis of such theories generally requires either (i) a rigorous procedure for quotienting out the symmetry (e.g., gauge fixing without resorting to perturbative Faddeev–Popov manipulations, or taking rigorous replica limits) or (ii) a controlled mechanism for symmetry breaking, in a way compatible with non-perturbative bounds (cluster or multiscale expansions, Borel summability, etc.). Moreover, the formal Ward identities expressing infinitesimal invariance of correlation functions have, in most non-trivial models, only been established at the level of formal power series.

Problem. Develop general non-perturbative methods, applicable within constructive field theory, to:

(a) rigorously quotient out gauge symmetry, supersymmetry, and replica symmetry in interacting Euclidean field theory models, producing well-defined measures (or correlation functions) on the quotient space and uniform non-perturbative estimates (e.g., stability bounds, convergent expansions, Borel summability) that survive the quotient/limit;

(b) rigorously implement controlled breakings of such symmetries (gauge fixing, soft supersymmetry breaking, finite-n replica setups followed by analytic continuation to n → 0) with explicit non-perturbative error control;

(c) establish a general theory of non-perturbative Ward identities — that is, exact identities among correlation functions of the constructed (non-perturbative) measure expressing the infinitesimal symmetries — together with criteria guaranteeing their validity in concrete constructive models (gauge theories, supersymmetric models, replica models of disordered systems).

Provide such methods at a level of generality that goes beyond model-by-model arguments, and apply them to representative examples (e.g., a non-Abelian lattice or continuum gauge theory, a supersymmetric matrix or sigma model, or a replica formulation of a disordered system).

